{% trace-scenario title="Which metric should drive the release?" prompt="A candidate model improves answer similarity but increases failed tool calls. How should the team decide?" outcome="Compare the complete task scorecard and inspect representative traces; do not promote a candidate on one convenient metric." /%}

## At a glance

This guide is for **engineers evaluating LLM and AI agent changes**. It helps you **choose metrics that support a release decision**. It does not claim that one score can represent every user, risk, or workflow.

### The evaluation scorecard

Use **Outcome → Quality → Reliability → Economics → Safety**. Start with the user task, then add dimensions that explain whether a change is acceptable.

## Measure the task first

Define what successful completion means and how failure affects the user. For a RAG answer, that may include groundedness and citation correctness. For a tool-using agent, it may include correct tool selection, argument validity, and side-effect accuracy. [AI agent evaluation](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) covers how to build that decision path.

| Dimension | Example question |
| --- | --- |
| Outcome | Did the user task finish correctly? |
| Quality | Was the answer relevant and supported? |
| Reliability | Did tools and dependencies behave? |
| Economics | What did the run cost and how long did it take? |
| Safety | Did the workflow stay inside policy? |

## Pair aggregate scores with traces

An average can hide a severe regression in a small cohort. Store evaluator version, candidate version, task distribution, sampled examples, and failure class. Inspect traces for the mechanism behind the score. [AI agent testing](/blog/ai-agent-testing-unit-tests-production-evals) shows how unit tests, regression cases, and production evaluations fit together.

## Set decision thresholds before looking

Write the baseline, acceptable regression, required improvement, and stop condition before comparing candidates. If the sample is small or the evaluator is noisy, state that uncertainty. A precise number without a stable sampling rule is not strong evidence.

## Release checklist

- Define the user-visible outcome.
- Choose metrics that explain quality, reliability, cost, latency, and safety.
- Version the candidate, evaluator, prompt, and dataset.
- Review representative failures, not only aggregate scores.
- Write promotion and rollback thresholds in advance.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What is the best AI evaluation metric?" answer="The best primary metric is the one that represents the user task. Add quality, reliability, cost, latency, and safety dimensions when they can change the release decision." /%}
{% faq-item question="Can an LLM judge evaluate another LLM?" answer="It can provide useful evidence, but validate its agreement with human or task-specific checks, monitor evaluator drift, and keep the evaluator version attached to every result." /%}
{% faq-item question="How many examples are needed?" answer="Enough to represent important cohorts and failure modes, with uncertainty reported honestly. A smaller, curated set is more useful than a larger set that excludes difficult cases." /%}

